## What does the model actually see?

Everything so far has been about the model: a program that turns text into probabilities for the
next token. But nobody chats with a bare model. Chat apps and coding agents wrap it in ordinary
code, the <GlossaryPopover term="harness">harness</GlossaryPopover>, which decides what text the
model gets and what to do with what it writes.

To see it, we recorded a real conversation with a small chat model, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, inside
a simple harness built for this site.<Cite id="qwen2024" /> You typed one short line. Peel back
the layers to see what the model actually read:

<PromptInspector />

Your 31-character message became a 400-token prompt. Most of it was written by the harness:

- a <GlossaryPopover term="system-prompt">system prompt</GlossaryPopover> with the app's
  instructions,
- descriptions of the tools the model may ask for,
- the earlier turns of the conversation, and
- the chat markers from Ch 13 around every turn.

The model has no memory between calls. It seems to remember the conversation only because the
harness sends the whole thing again every time. Here it also asked to look up a rhyme first, so the
harness called it a second time with the result added, and the prompt grew to 493 tokens.

## The model is one part

The reply you see is also the harness's work: it reads what the model wrote, spots tool calls,
runs them, and shows you only the final answer. The same model can feel very different depending
on the harness around it: its instructions, its tools, and the rules it enforces. The next four
chapters look at each job in turn.

<RecapCard>

- A chat app or agent is a model plus a harness: ordinary code around it.
- The harness builds the prompt: system prompt, tool descriptions, earlier turns, and chat markers.
- The model has no memory; the harness re-sends the conversation on every call.

</RecapCard>

